From 802c0fac1e09a57826bdfae0f1ddb027ccd6b9de Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matt C <latentcollapse@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 02:13:11 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 06/45] NIRA-Prime Phase 1: extract agent_observe/agent_message
 as host services
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two of seven confirmed-clean host-handler groups (from
PRIME_HOST_HANDLER_INVENTORY_2026-09-22.md's 26-handler count) are now
real, tested tools reachable without an ipython kernel, not just
theoretically substrate-neutral.

- AgentObserveController and AgentSessionMessageController were
  already standalone interfaces with zero AgentSession coupling — the
  only thing tying them to Python was that the model could only reach
  them through _createKernelHostHandlers, dispatched over the kernel's
  Jupyter comm channel. No business logic changed: the new tools call
  the exact same controller methods the kernel bridge already called.
- New tool files (agent-observe.ts, agent-message.ts) follow the
  existing bash.ts/neurabash.ts tool-definition pattern exactly —
  reusing Prime's own extension point (the tool registry) as the
  substrate-neutral boundary, not a new service framework.
- _buildRuntime merges these into _baseToolDefinitions when their
  controller is configured, for both the kernel path and the
  baseToolsOverride (NEURA) path — same tool, either mode, no
  duplication. Absent when no controller is set: no new default
  surface for sessions that never had these controllers.
- Errors are thrown, not returned via a nonexistent isError field on
  AgentToolResult (a real bug caught by tsgo, not a style choice) —
  matches bash.ts's actual error convention.

RLM subagent lifecycle (spawn/list/collect/delete/find_models) was
deliberately NOT touched. A dependency-mapping pass found real,
specific evidence it's invasive: _startRlmChildRun touches 20+ private
AgentSession fields, and spawning a child constructs a full recursive
AgentSession, not a lightweight principal object. This is not Python
coupling — it's coupling to AgentSession's own identity — but it means
extraction needs either formally generalizing AgentSession or a real
~400-line refactor, not a same-pass addition alongside six other
groups. Full evidence and the updated chassis-decision read in
NIRA_PRIME_HOST_SERVICES_V1_REPORT.md.

Async bash lifecycle (bash.completed/consumed) is a documented
substrate gap, not a host-coupling problem: the underlying injection
mechanism is already general-purpose and reused elsewhere, but
NeuraBash's tool is synchronous one-shot today with no async event to
plug into it. Left as-is rather than building a service with no
second real caller.

Tests: 8/8 passing, including a real IPython-breakout test (a faux
model explicitly attempts to call "ipython" in a NEURA session; the
call is rejected as an error, never reaches real ipython logic — there
is none registered to reach).

